4.13.2. Velocity of centre of mass:
Let v_1, v_2, ... v_n be the velocities of a system of point masses m_1, m_2, ... m_n. Velocity of the centre of mass of the system is given by
\overrightarrow{\mathbf{v}}_{\mathrm{cm}} = \frac{\sum_{1}^{n} m_{i} \overrightarrow{\mathbf{v}}_{i}}{\sum_{1}^{n} m_{i}} = \frac{\sum_{1}^{n} m_{i} \overrightarrow{\mathbf{v}}_{i}}{M}
= \frac{\text{resultant linear momentum}}{\text{total mass}}
= weighted average of momenta
x, y and z components of \vec{v} can be obtained similarly.
For continuous distribution, \vec{v}_{cm} = \frac{\int \vec{v} dm}{M}
4.13.3. Acceleration of the centre of mass:
Let a_1, a_2, ... a_n be the accelerations of a system of point masses m_1, m_2, ... m_n. Acceleration of the centre of mass of the system is given by
\vec{a}_{cm} = \frac{\sum_{1}^{n} m_{i} \vec{a}_{i}}{\sum_{1}^{n} m_{i}} = \frac{\sum_{1}^{n} m_{i} \vec{a}_{i}}{M}
= \frac{\text{resultant force}}{\text{total mass}}
= weighted average of forces
x, y and z components of \vec{a} can be obtained similarly.
For continuous distribution, \vec{a}_{cm} = \frac{\int \vec{a} \ dm}{M}
